{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "log-04-change-of-base-identities",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Change of Base and Logarithm Identities",
    "description": "Convert logarithms between valid bases and use reciprocal and cyclic identities without losing domain conditions.",
    "teachingApproach": "Develop definitions and laws step by step, explain why each transformation is valid, show intermediate calculations and checks, and use targeted practice with changed-value remedial retries.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 50,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 10 “Logarithms,” printed pages 297–307. Approved scope: Develop log_a x=log_b x/log_b a and its common-log form, then use reciprocal identity log_a b=1/log_b a and product/cycle identities such as log_a b·log_b c=log_a c. Cover expressions with multiple bases, conversion to a convenient prime or common base, and supplied logarithm values. Explain that all bases must be positive and not 1 and all logged arguments positive. Avoid dividing by a logarithm that may be zero without checking its base/argument conditions."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Welcome to Change of Base and Logarithm Identities. Convert logarithms between valid bases and use reciprocal and cyclic identities without losing domain conditions.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_1": {
      "id": "lesson_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A logarithm can be changed to a more convenient base. Starting with y=log_a(x), write a^y=x. Taking log_b of both positive sides gives y log_b(a)=log_b(x), hence log_a(x)=log_b(x)/log_b(a). The denominator is nonzero because a≠1.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_2"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_2": {
      "id": "lesson_2",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The common-log version is log_a(x)=log_10(x)/log_10(a). It is useful when a calculator or a supplied table gives base-10 values. The same formula works with any allowed base b, provided b>0 and b≠1.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_3"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_3": {
      "id": "lesson_3",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The reciprocal identity follows immediately: log_a(b)·log_b(a)=1, so log_a(b)=1/log_b(a). Both logarithms must exist. Their values cannot be zero because their arguments are bases different from 1.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_4"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_4": {
      "id": "lesson_4",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A chain of bases telescopes: log_a(b)·log_b(c)=log_a(c). The middle change cancels because log_a(b)=ln(b)/ln(a) and log_b(c)=ln(c)/ln(b); the ln(b) factors cancel. The same result follows from rewriting c=b^(log_b c), then b=a^(log_a b).",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_5"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_5": {
      "id": "lesson_5",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Long cycles collapse. log_a(b)·log_b(c)·log_c(a)=1 when all bases are positive and not 1. A cyclic chain that ends where it began returns to the starting logarithm value 1.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_6"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_6": {
      "id": "lesson_6",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Be careful with reciprocals of arguments and reciprocals of bases. log_a(1/x)=−log_a(x), while log_(1/a)(x)=−log_a(x) for x>0 and a valid positive base a. They are related sign changes but arise from different positions in the notation.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_7"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_7": {
      "id": "lesson_7",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Example: evaluate log_4(8). Change to base 2: log_4(8)=log_2(8)/log_2(4)=3/2. Check 4^(3/2)=(sqrt4)^3=8.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_8"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_8": {
      "id": "lesson_8",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Example: simplify log_2(3)·log_3(5)·log_5(8). The first two factors combine to log_2(5), and multiplying by log_5(8) gives log_2(8)=3. Each middle base cancels exactly.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_9"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_9": {
      "id": "lesson_9",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If supplied values are in a different base, change both numerator and denominator consistently. For log_5(12), use log_10(12)/log_10(5); do not divide by log_10(12) or change only one part.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_10"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_10": {
      "id": "lesson_10",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The formula cannot use a base-change denominator log_b(a)=0. That would imply a=1, which is excluded as a target logarithm base. Always check this before using the quotient.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_11"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_11": {
      "id": "lesson_11",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A common trap is reversing the change-of-base fraction. log_a(x)=log_b(x)/log_b(a), not the reverse. Test with x=a: the numerator and denominator are equal, so the result is 1.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_12"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_12": {
      "id": "lesson_12",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Another trap is assuming log_a(b)=log_b(a). In general they are reciprocals, not equal. For example log_2(8)=3 while log_8(2)=1/3.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_13"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_13": {
      "id": "lesson_13",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "All arguments and bases must remain in the real domain during a chain. If an intermediate symbol is later used as a base, it must also be positive and not 1; if it is used as an argument, it must be positive.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_14"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_14": {
      "id": "lesson_14",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Use change of base when bases differ, a cyclic product appears, or the supplied log values use another base. If the base and argument are simple powers of a common number, direct exponent comparison is usually shorter.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_15"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_15": {
      "id": "lesson_15",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Check a result either by raising the original base to the proposed logarithm value or by expanding the change-of-base ratio. Exact cancellation is safer than relying on rounded decimals.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_16"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_16": {
      "id": "lesson_16",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Derive the formula by setting y=log_a(x). Then a^y=x. Taking log base b gives y log_b(a)=log_b(x), and division yields y=log_b(x)/log_b(a). This derivation explains why the original base appears in the denominator.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_17"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_17": {
      "id": "lesson_17",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For example, if log_10(12)=1.0792 and log_10(5)=0.6990 are supplied, then log_5(12)=1.0792/0.6990≈1.544. This is an approximation because the supplied common logs are rounded; the exact change-of-base ratio is preferable when available.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_18"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_18": {
      "id": "lesson_18",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The reciprocal identity is a special change-of-base result: log_a(b)=log_b(b)/log_b(a)=1/log_b(a), because log_b(b)=1. It is valid only when both logarithms exist, so a and b must each be positive and different from 1.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_19"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_19": {
      "id": "lesson_19",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A cyclic product returns to its starting point. In log_a(b)·log_b(c)·log_c(a), the first two factors combine to log_a(c), and the last gives log_c(a); these are reciprocals, so the product is 1.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lesson_20"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lesson_20": {
      "id": "lesson_20",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Do not telescope a sum of logs as though it were a product. log_2(3)+log_3(5) does not simplify by cancelling the 3; the chain identity applies to multiplication of adjacent logarithms, not addition.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "focus_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "focus_1": {
      "id": "focus_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Checkpoint 1: Change of Base and Logarithm Identities. The change-of-base fraction is log of the argument over log of the base. Read the full prompt, identify the relevant definition or law, and check the domain before choosing.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
